WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.900
Whether it's swimming, splashing, or 
rolling around in the mud,

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.520
there's nothing an elephant loves more than bath time.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.820
This elephant water park isn't just for fun, though.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:22.060
Temperatures in the hot African savanna average around 85 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.680
But staying cool is no problem for elephants,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.440
thanks to millions of microscopic cracks
in their skin.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.940
How do elephants get their cracks?

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.840
And why does it help them beat the heat?

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.820
The African elephant is the largest living
land animal in the world.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:57.100
It can grow up to 13 feet high and weigh up to 7 tons.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.700
Its outer skin layer is about 50 times
 as thick as a human's.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.280
But, unlike many mammals, elephants don't sweat.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.960
They control much of their body temperature through evaporative cooling—

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.080
which requires the wetting of the skin through regular bathing and spraying.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.680
Elephants can store up to two and a half gallons
of water in their trunk at a time.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.100
They use their amazing sense of smell to find water from miles away—

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:35.400
even when it's inside a tree or below ground.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.960
Unlike humans, elephants don't shed their dead skin.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.320
When baby elephants are born,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.860
their skin is covered in tiny protrusions called papillae.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.120
These are similar to the small kinds of bumps 
that are found on the human tongue.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.760
As they get older, their skin cells build up thicker and thicker over the dermis.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.360
Eventually, these accumulated layers start to bend under
pressure,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.000
causing deep cracks to form in between the papillae.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.060
Water then flows
through the crevices using capillary action,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.380
which is the same force that allows 
plant roots to soak up water from the soil.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:28.780
This process transforms the skin surface
 into an intricate network of channels.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:37.680
As a result, elephant skin can hold up to ten times 
more water than a smooth surface.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:43.160
Their fractured skin also helps to keep mud and dust from sliding off.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:50.840
Like an all-natural sunblock, helping to protect elephants from sunburn and parasites.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:58.900
There is still more to learn about the unique ways that elephants have adapted to beat the heat.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.460
And scientists hope that understanding elephant skin could even help to improve treatments

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.280
for human skin conditions.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.280
Who knew elephant skin could be so cool?

